  "major": "international-business-bs-nyc-e54159c3",
  "major_name": "International Business",
  "degree": "BS",
  "campus": "New York",
  "college": "D'Amore-McKim School of Business",
  "overview_text": "The Bachelor of Science in International Business program is designed to prepare students to be successful managers in an evolving global economy. Students have an opportunity to develop the skills and knowledge needed to work across cultures and differing business practices, both virtually and face-to-face. Program curriculum examines how a global environment impacts a student's chosen concentration. Knowledge about other cultures and practices is best learned on location and experientially. Bachelor of Science in International Business students are expected to complete at least one semester of study or cooperative education experience in a country other than where they grew up. Students will create an individual academic plan based upon their interest and career goals. Students are encouraged to become proficient in a second language. Upon graduation, students are able to make an impact from day one at companies that are engaged in business internationally. Graduates can also work in their home country for a company that operates internationally as well as overseas on a foreign assignment. This program is currently only available to former Marymount Manhattan College students who are eligible for continued enrollment at Northeastern University. This program includes required courses that will be taught on ground at the Northeastern New York City campus.",
  "source_text": "Complete all courses listed below unless otherwise indicated. Also complete any corequisite labs, recitations, clinicals, or tools courses where specified and complete any additional courses needed beyond specific college and major requirements to satisfy graduation credit requirements.\nUniversitywide Requirements\nAll undergraduate students are required to complete the\nUniversitywide Requirements\n.\nNUpath Requirements\nAll undergraduate students are required to complete the\nNUpath Requirements\n.\nNUpath requirements Differences and Diversity (DD), Natural and Designed World (ND), and Formal and Quantitative Reasoning (FQ) are not explicitly satisfied by required courses in the business curriculum at this time. Students are responsible for satisfying these requirements, and if these are not fulfilled in required major courses, they should use general electives to do so.\nNew York State Liberal Arts and Sciences Requirements\nAll undergraduate students enrolled in programs at the New York campus are required to fulfill\nNew York State Liberal Arts and Sciences requirements\n.\nBachelor of Science in International Business students are required to complete at least a one-semester overseas requirement. The one semester overseas can be fulfilled by a study-abroad, two international summer dialogues, one N.U.in international experience, one international Global Scholars semester, or overseas co-op.\nBusiness Core\nCourse List\nCode\nTitle\nHours\nACCT 1201\nFinancial Accounting and Reporting\n4\nFINA 2201\nFinancial Management\n4\nINNO 2301\nInnovation!\n4\nMGSC 2301\nBusiness Statistics\n4\nMKTG 2201\nIntroduction to Marketing\n4\nORGB 3201\nOrganizational Behavior\n4\nor\nORGB 3203\nBehavioral Aspects of Digital Transformation\nInternational Business Requirements\nCourse List\nCode\nTitle\nHours\nAll Bachelor of Science in International Business core courses must be completed at D'Amore-McKim or one of its approved partner universities.\nINTB 1205\nThe Global Business Environment\n4\nINTB 2207\nDecision Making in Developed and Emerging Markets\n4\nINTB 3205\nUnderstanding and Managing Cultural Differences\n4\nor\nINTB 3310\nCultural Aspects of International Business\nINTB 4202\nExecuting Global Strategy\n4\nComplete one of the following international electives:\n4\nFINA 4320\nInternational Financial Management\nINTB 2501\nCompeting to Win in Emerging Markets\nINTB 3330\nDigital Globalization\nINTB 4983\nSpecial Topics in International Business\nMKTG 4512\nInternational Marketing\nSCHM 3301\nGlobal Supply Chain Strategy\nSTRT 3301\nMultinational Corporations and Global Sustainability: Complexities, Strategies, and Societal Impact\nInternational Experiential Learning\nCourse List\nCode\nTitle\nHours\nComplete one of the following:\nOne semester of study-abroad experience\nTwo Dialogues of Civilization (summer programs)\nTwo summer traditional study-abroad experiences\nOne N.U.in international experience or one international Global Scholars semester\nCOOP 3948\nCo-op Work Experience Abroad\n1\nGBST 5101\nGlobal Network Program—International\n1\nCo-op Work Experience Abroad (\nCOOP 3948\n)\nmay also be used toward the business experiential learning requirement.\nConcentration\nComplete one of the following required concentrations.  A second concentration is optional. Certain concentrations require additional prerequisites. Please consult your academic advisor to ensure you have met these requirements.\nEntrepreneurial Startups\nFashion Marketing\nFinance\nManagement\nMarketing\nMedia and Arts Management\nSupporting Courses\nCourse List\nCode\nTitle\nHours\nEconomics Requirement\nECON 1116\nPrinciples of Microeconomics\n4\nPersonal and Professional Development\nBUSN 1102\nPersonal Skill Development for Business\n1\nBUSN 1103\nProfessional Development for Business Co-op\n1\nor\nEXED 2000\nProfessional Development for Co-op\nBusiness Experiential Learning\nCourse List\nCode\nTitle\nHours\nComplete at least one of the following:\n0-8\nBUSN 4945\nExperiential Professional Practicum\nCOOP 3945\nCo-op Work Experience\nCOOP 3946\nCo-op Work Experience--Half Time\nCOOP 3947\nCo-op Work Experience Abroad--Half Time\nCOOP 3948\nCo-op Work Experience Abroad\nEEBA 2945\nInternship Experience (with D’Amore-McKim Cooperative Education Department Approval)\nEEBA 2948\nInternship Experience Abroad (with D’Amore-McKim Cooperative Education Department Approval)\nBusiness GPA Requirement\nA minimum 2.000 GPA in business courses is required.\nMajor Credit Requirement\nComplete 66 semester hours in the major.\nProgram Requirement\n128 total semester hours required",
